# Sandwich-Like Fe&TiO2@C Nanocomposites Derived from MXene/Fe-MOFs Hybrids for Electromagnetic Absorption

## abstract
Electromagnetic pollution has been causing a series of problems in people's life, and electromagnetic absorbers with lightweight and broad absorbing bandwidth properties are widely desired. In this work, novel sandwich-like 2D laminated Fe&TiO2 nanoparticles@C nanocomposites were rationally designed and successfully developed from the MXene-MOFs hybrids. The formation of Fe and rutile-TiO2 nanoparticles sandwiched by the two-dimensional carbon nanosheets provided strong electromagnetic energy attenuation and good impedance matching for electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorption. As expected, the nanocomposites achieved a broad effective absorption bandwidth of 6.5 GHz at a thickness of only 1.6 mm and the minimum reflection loss (RL) value of - 51.8 dB at 6.6 GHz with a thickness of 3 mm. This work not only provides a good design and fabricating concept for the laminated metal and functional nanoparticles@C nanocomposites with good EMW absorption, but also offers an important guideline to fabricate various two-dimensional nanocomposites derived from the MXene precursors.

Sandwich‑Like Fe $\mathbf{\lambda}_{\mathbf{k}}\mathbf{TiO}_{2}@\mathbf{C}$ Nanocomposites Derived from MXene/Fe‑MOFs Hybrids for Electromagnetic Absorption
## HIGHLIGHTS
•	 Sandwich-like laminated Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\cal C}$ nanocomposites were successfully prepared from the MXene–MOFs hybrids.
A broad effective bandwidth of $6.5\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of only $1.6\mathrm{mm}$ was achieved.
ABSTRACT  Electromagnetic pollution has been causing a series of problems in people’s life, and electromagnetic absorbers with lightweight and broad absorbing bandwidth properties are widely desired. In this work, novel sandwich-like 2D laminated Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $\ @\mathrm{C}$ nanocomposites were rationally designed and successfully developed from the MXene–MOFs hybrids. The formation of Fe and rutile- $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles sandwiched by the two-dimensional carbon nanosheets pro‑ vided strong electromagnetic energy attenuation and good impedance matching for electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorption. As expected, the nanocomposites achieved a broad effective absorption bandwidth of $6.5\:\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of only $1.6\mathrm{mm}$ and the minimum reflection loss $(R L)$ value of − 51.8 dB at $6.6\mathrm{GHz}$ with a thickness of $3\mathrm{mm}$ . This work not only provides a good design and fabricating concept for the laminated metal and functional nanoparticles $\textstyle{\mathcal{@}}\mathbf{C}$ nanocomposites with good EMW absorption, but also offers an important guideline to fabricate various two-dimensional nanocomposites derived from the MXene precursors.
KEYWORDS  MXene; Metal–organic frameworks; Nanocomposites; Electromagnetic wave absorption
## 1  Introduction
With the surging development of electrical communication devices, an extensive increase in hazardous electromagnetic radiation and interferes has become a severe problem in both civil and precision technical fields. Therefore, there is a great demand for developing broadband and high-efficiency electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorbing materials to mini‑ mize adverse effects in aspects of health and electrical devices [1–3]. Abundant efforts have been contributed to the development of high-efficiency EMW absorbers with a broad effective absorption bandwidth (EAB), especially for the radar frequency range of 2–18 GHz [4–6]. In general, mate‑ rials with laminated structure are well known as promising
EMW absorbers. Due to the unique layered structure of these laminated materials, which possess large surface area and heterogeneous interfaces, abundant dipolar, and interfacial polarizations could be generated during EMW absorption process. This is believed to be the main contribution to the excellent EMW absorbing ability [7–9]. Specifically, the most widely studied laminated EMW absorbers was the gra‑ phene-based two-dimensional materials in the past decade [2, 9–11]. Since the EMW absorption ability of graphene or reduced graphene oxide (RGO) nanosheets was not ideal, assembling functional particles with graphene or RGO has become a promising way to improve their performance, such as Ni/graphene [12], $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-rGO}$ [13], and $Z{\mathrm{nO-rGO}}$ [14].
MXene is a new family member of the big family tree of two-dimensional (2D) materials [15, 16], whose for‑ mula is $M_{n+1}X_{n}T_{x},$ where $M$ is an early transition metal, $X$ is carbon and/or nitrogen, and $T_{x}$ is the surface terminations usually $-\mathrm{OH}$ , –F, and/or $^{-0}$ [17–19]. Due to the laminated structure and unique combination of electrical conductiv‑ ity and hydrophilicity, MXene is considered as an attractive candidate for various applications, including energy stor‑ age [16, 20, 21], sensors [22, 23], electromagnetic absorp‑ tion, and electromagnetic interference shielding [3, 8, 24, 25]. Feng et al. [8] synthesized $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ nanosheets by HF system etching process, which appeared enhanced micro‑ wave absorption ability ( $R L$ value of $-40\mathrm{dB}$ at $2\mathrm{mm}$ thick‑ ness). $\mathrm{{Xu}}$ et al. [26] achieved a $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ with a $R L$ value of $-41.9~\mathrm{dB}$ at $13.4~\mathrm{GHz}$ and thickness of only $1.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ by solvothermal treatment. Luo et al. [5] demonstrated a wide absorbing bandwidth ( $E A B$ over $10\:\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of $3\mathrm{mm}$ ) in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ MXene absorbers. $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ is a representa‑ tive of MXene family decorated with abundant active sur‑ face functional terminations [27–29], which could provide remarkable amount of dipolar and interfacial polarizations. Moreover, the variety surface terminations of MXene by ion intercalation [30], annealing [31], and oxidization [32] could offer tunable EMW absorbing properties. In general, EMW absorbers should have strong EM energy attenuation ability and moderate conductivity for impedance matching. However, the high electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ could be a big obstacle for the application as EMW absorbers [32]. Generally, the combination of magnetic particles and conductive carbon materials is considered as an effective way to achieve an improved EMW absorption by improv‑ ing the impedance matching [33, 34]. Therefore, assembling the magnetic nanoparticles with the MXene oxides, such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [6], $\mathrm{NiO}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{C}$ [35], $\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\alpha$ –Fe [36], and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{C}$ [37], could effectively improve the impedance matching with little cost of its EMW energy attenuation performance. In summary, integrating magnetic particles with oxidized MXene could be a promising and effective way to obtain high-performance EMW absorption materials by optimizing the impedance matching.
The magnetic particles could be derived from novel metal organic framework (MOFs) materials [38–41]. Owing to their unique porous structures, MOFs are considered as ideal precursors for preparing nanostructured metal $\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{c}$ nanocomposites through facile heat treatment methods and provide intriguing electromagnetic (EM) absorption proper‑ ties [42–45]. For instance, Zhou et al. [44] fabricated hier‑ archical MOF-derived $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hollow spheres with an enhanced EMW absorption performance $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ of − 40.1 dB and EBW of $4.64\:\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of $1.5\mathrm{mm}$ ). Liu et al. [46] synthesized porous carbon-wrapped Ni composites from Ni-based MOF with high EMW absorption perfor‑ mance $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ of $-51.8~\mathrm{dB}$ and an EBW of $3.48\:\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of $2.6\mathrm{mm}$ ). Lü et al. [47] provided a porous $\mathrm{Co/C}$ nanocomposite derived from ZIF-67 (a kind of Co-based MOF), which achieved a broad EBW of $5.8~\mathrm{GHz}$ with a $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ value of − 35.3 dB. Li et al. [48] also successfully prepared hollow $\mathrm{Co/C}$ microspheres from ZIF-67, which exhibited a high $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ of $-66.5$ dB. Thus, the combination of 2D materials and MOFs derived magnetic particles could be a solution for achieving high-performance and broadband EMW absorbers. Though there are numerous works about introducing magnetic materials into MXene as mentioned, the EMW absorbers fabricated by in situ MXene–MOFs hybrids have been rarely studied [3, 37].
Herein, we demonstrate a facile route toward the rational construction of sandwich-like 2D laminated Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $@\mathrm{C}$ nanocomposites derived from MXene/FeMOFs hybrids through a rapid microwave-assisted heating reaction followed by a suitable heat treatment. The in situ generated Fe and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles embedded closely into each MXene-derived carbon nanolayers to form a twodimensional sandwich-like laminated structure. The results indicate that the 2D laminated nanocomposites exhibit excellent tunable EMW absorption performance with high reflection loss ( $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ of − 51.8 dB), lightweight (a match‑ ing thickness of $1.6~\mathrm{mm}$ ), and broad bandwidth $(6.6\:\mathrm{GHz})$ due to the plentiful interfacial polarization and other mecha‑ nisms of EMW attenuation. Furthermore, our work offers an important facile approach to fabricate two-dimensional nanocomposites derived from MXene–MOF hybrids along with a good design and fabricating concept for the laminated metal and functional nanoparticles $@\mathrm{C}$ nanocomposites with good EMW absorption.
## 2  Experimental Section
## 2.1  Raw Materials
In this work, all chemical reagents were of analytical grade and not further purified. Specifically, hydrochloric acid (HCl), lithium fluoride (LiF), $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ , and ferric chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Industrial Corporation. $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ powders $(>98~\mathrm{wt}\%$ purity) were purchased from Laizhou Kai Kai Ceramic Materials Co., Ltd.
## 2.2  Preparation of $\mathbf{Ti}_{3}\mathbf{C}_{2}\pmb{T}_{x}$ ‑MXene
$\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ MXene was synthesized by removing Al atoms in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ precursor based on the typical LiF–HCl etching method [19]. Firstly, $0.5\mathrm{~g~}$ of LiF was added into the con‑ centrated $12\mathrm{~M~HCl}$ solution. Secondly, the solution was stirred $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ with a magnetic Teflon stir to dissolve the LiF completely. Then, $0.5\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ was slowly added into the solution and stirred at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The resultant suspension was washed by deionized water for several times via centrifugation process ( $3500\mathrm{rpm}$ , 5 min for every wash‑ ing circle) until the $\mathrm{pH}$ value of supernatant became 5–6. Finally, the obtained $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ sediments were collected and dried in a vacuum oven for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.3  Preparation of MXene–MOF Hybrids
The $0.12~\mathrm{g}$ of as-prepared $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ MXene was added into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and then soni‑ cated for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ to form a uniform black solution. After that, $0.5405\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in the solution with sonication for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The $0.3323\ \mathrm{g}$ of terephthalic acid was dissolved into the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ mixture by vigor‑ ously stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ . After that, $0.8~\mathrm{mL}$ of $2\mathrm{MNaOH}$ solution was added dropwise into the mixture with ongoing stirring. Then, the as-prepared mixture was transferred into a $100\mathrm{-mL}$ three-neck boiling flask. Following that, the reac‑ tion was conducted at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{s}$ in a microwave heating equipment. The obtained black solution was centrifuged and washed by DMF, deionized water, and ethanol. The resulting black product was dried in a vacuum oven for $3\mathrm{h}$ . The mass of resultant $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ –Fe-MOFs hybrids was $0.26\mathrm{g}$ , indicat‑ ing the mass ratio for $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ and Fe-MOFs was about 1.2.
## 2.4  Preparation of Fe& $\mathbf{TiO}_{2}@\mathbf{C}$ Composites
The MXene–MOF hybrids obtained as above were carbon‑ ized at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ with a heating rate of $10{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ under a $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ atmosphere. This product will be referred as S7. To ascertain the influences of structure modification on EMW absorption ability, the MXene–MOF hybrids were also car‑ bonized at 600 and $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under the same atmosphere con‑ dition, which will be referred as S6 and S8, respectively.
## 2.5  Characterization
The thermogravimetric and differential scanning calorimetry (TG/DSC) curves of MXene–MOF hybrids were obtained to help understanding the transforming process from $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ –Fe-MOF hybrids to $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ composites, by using a thermal analysis system at the temperature range from 30 to $850~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the obtained S6, S7, S8, and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ sam‑ ples were recorded by X-ray diffractometer equipped with $\mathrm{CuK}_{\upalpha}$ radiation from $4^{\circ}$ to $65^{\circ}$ (a step scan of $0.02^{\circ}$ and 1 s per step). Raman tests were carried out on a Raman micro‑ spectrometer with a He–Ne laser. The magnetic properties of the composites were measured by vibrating sample mag‑ netometer (VSM, Lake Shore7307). The morphologies of the precursor and obtained powders were characterized by a transmission electron microscope (TEM) and a scan electron microscope (SEM).
To measure complex permittivity and complex perme‑ ability, paraffin was chosen as the matrix material due to its small contribution to dielectric and magnetic properties. S6, S7, and S8 samples were dispersed into the paraffin with loading ratio of $40\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ . Finally, the well-mixed mixtures were molded into a torus-shape ( $3.04\mathrm{mm}$ of inner diameter and $7.00\mathrm{mm}$ of outer diameter) test samples. The complex permittivity and complex permeability were measured using an Agilent N5224A vector network analyzer in the frequency range from 2 to $18\mathrm{GHz}$ .
## 3  Results and Discussion
## 3.1  Synthesis of Fe& $\mathbf{TiO}_{2}@\mathbf{C}$ Nanocomposites
The detailed fabrication process of the sandwich-like two-dimensional laminated $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets composites is illustrated in Fig. 1. Firstly, the 2D laminated MXene nanosheets were synthesized based on a typical LiF-HCl etching method. Then, the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ –FeMOFs hybrids were facilely synthesized by the microwaveassisted heating process using $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , terephthalic acid, and $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ as reactants in a DMF solution. During the reaction process, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions, which were inserted in or absorbed on the layers of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x},$ could form a shut‑ tle-like Fe-MOFs by coordinating the carboxylate groups of terephthalic acid by strong metal–ligand bonds [49]. Finally, the sandwich-like two-dimensional $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ nanocomposites were obtained via the carbonization of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ –Fe-MOFs hybrids at high temperature for $2\mathrm{h}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ Ar atmosphere. In the carbonization process, shuttle-like FeMOFs have been transformed into Fe nanoparticles covered with carbon. At the same time, the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles and laminated carbon nanosheets were in situ generated from the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ . Therefore, this transformation process provided a novel sandwich-like two-dimensional laminated structure of $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{C}$ nanocomposites, in which the layered carbon nanosheets were filled with the Fe and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles.
## 3.2  Microstructure
The TG curve of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ –Fe-MOF hybrids is shown in Fig. 2a, while the DSC result is exhibited in Fig. S1. Three significant weight loss processes were observed from the TG curve. The first process, ranging from 30 to $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , could be considered as a weight loss process for the non-combined water in hybrids. The following weight loss at temperature range of 115 to $340^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was attributed to the loss of the com‑ bined water and surface functional groups [32]. The total weight loss from 30 to $340^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was $18.2\%$ . Another weight loss occurred at the temperature range of 340 to $570~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which accompanied with an exothermic peak at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , might be attributed to the carbonization of Fe-MOF and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ . It was worth noting that a sharp exothermic peak was found at $566~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the DSC curve, which could be the result for phase transformation from anatase to rutile. The XRD patterns of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ –FeMOFs hybrids carbonized at 600, 700, and $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are demonstrated in Fig. 2b. The typical peaks corresponding to the (002), (004), and (006) planes of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ could be seen at $6.7^{\circ}$ , $13.7^{\circ}$ , and $18.7^{\circ}$ , respectively, suggesting the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ MXene [19, 50, 51]. It was seen that a small peak at $25.74^{\circ}$ appeared after carboniz‑ ing at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which was corresponding to the (101) plane of anatase- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ (JCPDS No. 75-1537). The other peaks at $27.7^{\circ}$ , $36.3^{\circ}$ , $41.5^{\circ}$ , $54.5^{\circ}$ , and $56.84^{\circ}$ in the XRD pat‑ tern of S6 were assigned to the (110), (101), (111), (211), and (220) planes of the rutile- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ (JCPDS No. 78-1510). Moreover, the peak at $44.9^{\circ}$ was indexed to the (110) plane of Fe (JCPDS No. 87-0722). As shown in Fig.  2b, the anatase phase disappeared when the temperature increased over $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which agreed with the results reported in other works [31, 52, 53]. With the temperature increasing to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , there were only rutile- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ and Fe phase remained in the sample. The phase fraction and averaged grain size of all the three samples (S6, S7, and S8) were calculated based on the XRD data by using Jade software with the WPF refinement, and the results are summarized in Table 1. The fractions of iron phase kept around $23\%$ when the carboniza‑ tion temperature increased from 600 to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating the Fe element in Fe-MOFs was completely reduced and transformed into zerovalent metallic state by hydrogen. On the other hand, the sum of phase fractions of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in all the three samples was close to $75\%$ , suggesting the Ti element in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ sheets was in the form of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The averaged grain size of rutile- $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ increased from 40.1 to $57.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{nm}}$ with the increasing carbonizing temperature. The formation of rutile- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the disappearance of anatase- $\mathbf{\cdot}\Gamma\mathrm{i}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ could be beneficial to the dielectric loss since the electromagnetic response of latter phase is worse than that of the former [53]. In addition, the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ is provided in Fig. S2. The Raman spectra of the three samples (S6, S7, and S8) are shown in Fig. 2c. The peaks, which corresponded to the characteristic D and G bands of carbon, appeared at 1343 and $1597~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the Raman spectra of S6, S7, and
S8. The intensities ratio between $\mathrm{~D~}$ and G $(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ of S6, S7, and S8 was 0.89, 0.86, and 0.91, respectively. Gener‑ ally, the descending value of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ indicates that the amor‑ phous carbon transforms into the ordered graphite carbon [28]. On the one hand, the decreasing $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ values for S6 to S7 samples could imply the improved carbon ordering with the increasing temperature. Normally, the amount of defects would be reduced with the increasing temperature, which led to the decreasing $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ value and the transforma‑ tion from amorphous carbon to ordered carbon [54]. On the other hand, the $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ values showed an increasing tendency from S7 to S8. This result could be interpreted as being caused by an increase in the number, or in the size, of the graphitic domains [55–57]. It was worth mentioning that the increasing order of carbon is beneficial to increase the conductivity of carbon sheets, which could contribute to the conductive loss in EMW absorption process [35].
The typical layered structure of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ is clearly exhib‑ ited in Fig.  3a [51]. Additionally, the morphologies of S6, S7, and S8 observed by SEM are shown in Fig. 3b–d. The similar 2D laminated structure and small particles inserted between the layers were found in both S6 (Fig. 3b) and S7 (Fig. 3c). As for S8, it could be seen that the par‑ ticles between layers grew significant and showed a high degree of aggregation in Fig. 3d. The EDS result for asprepared $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ presented that the residual Al element was only $2.8~\mathrm{wt}\%$ , which confirmed the successful etch‑ ing of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ , as shown in Fig. S4. The element mapping analysis for S7 further illustrated the uniform distribution of the Fe, Ti, C, and O elements in the sandwich-like 2D $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ composites, as shown in Fig. S5. The TEM images of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ as-synthesized S6, S7, and S8 are presented in Fig. 3e–h. Before the carbonization process, it was seen that the shuttle-like Fe-MOFs, whose size was about $200{-}300\mathrm{nm}$ in length and $100{-}150\mathrm{nm}$ in width, were distributed homogenously in the laminated thin $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{X}$ sheets as shown in Fig. S3. After carbonating at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the smooth $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{X}$ sheets were transformed into coarse dis‑ ordered carbon sheets while rectangle $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles were precipitated. In addition, in our previous work, the microstructure evaluation of the Fe-MOFs during carboni‑ zation was studied [58]. Firstly, the Fe-MOFs transformed into $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles and porous carbon. Then, it began to collapse and transform into Fe nanoparticles with increas‑ ing the temperature. Due to the reduction atmosphere and relative longer carbonization time applied in this work, the
Fe-MOFs decomposed into Fe nanoparticles wrapped by carbon, as shown in Fig. 3f. The $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathbf{C}$ played an important role in improving impendence matching and providing inter‑ facial polarization and magnetic loss. When the tempera‑ ture increased to $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the size of the derived $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and Fe nanoparticles slightly increased, and the aggregation of nanoparticles also became more intense (Fig. 3g). Further‑ more, the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and Fe nanoparticles grew up excessively at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which was consistent with the XRD results shown in Table 1, and ended up in oversized particles, as exhibited in Fig. 3h. This sandwich-like 2D laminated structure of $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ composites was beneficial to the absorption of EM energy [37, 53].
## 3.3  Electromagnetic Parameters and Microwave Absorption Performance
The electromagnetic absorption of $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ composites was determined by the relative complex permittivity $(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}})$ and permeability $(\mu_{\mathrm{r}})$ . Normally, the real parts of permittivity and permeability $\cdot\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime}$ ) represent for the storage abil‑ ity of electromagnetic energy, while the imaginary parts of those $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ ) stand for the energy dissipation. Figure 4a, b shows the complex permittivity of $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ compos‑ ites derived at different temperatures at the frequency rang‑ ing from 2 to $18~\mathrm{{GHz}}$ . Both the real and imaginary parts of permittivity of $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ composites were enhanced with the increasing carbonizing temperatures. In detail, the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ value of S6 maintained constant at around 6.0 within the whole frequency range, indicating the poor energy storage ability. However, S7 and S8 exhibited much higher value of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ than S6 $\sim12$ for S7 and $\sim13$ for S8), which probably were resulted from the disappearance of the anatase- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the formation of more rutile- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ when the carbonat‑ ing temperature reached over $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ according to the XRD results. Besides, the mildly enlarged $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ value of S7 and S8 within the frequency was also helpful for achieving excel‑ lent EMW absorption. Similarly, the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ value of S6 stayed around 0.5 while that of S7 declined from 4.7 to 3.5 and S8 slowly decreased from 7.2 and then mildly fluctuated at around 4.6. Compared with S6, the S7 and S8 with higher $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values exhibited stronger EMW attenuation ability. In addi‑ tion, the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ plots of S7 exhibited an obvious fluctua‑ tion with the frequency ranging from 12 to $16\mathrm{GHz}$ , reveal‑ ing the existence of polarization behavior. As for S6, the low graphitization degree, which was identified by the Raman spectra results, caused the low values of permittivity leading to the flat $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ curves [59]. The $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ curves for S8 at the frequency range of $12{\mathrm{-}}16\mathrm{GHz}$ were mildly fluctuated, which could be attributed to the aggregation of nanoparti‑ cles hindering the polarization process. It was reasonable to assume that these dielectric properties mainly come from the polarization process, which were induced by the defects, the heterointerfaces, and the conductive carbon sheets existed in the $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ nanocomposites.
The magnetic hysteresis loops of all the three samples showed typical ferromagnetic features, as shown in Fig. S6a, b. The saturation magnetization $(M_{\mathrm{s}})$ of 19.7, 29.3, and 28.7 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and coercivity $(H_{\mathrm{c}})$ of 72.7, 84.7, and 37.8 Oe were corresponded to the S6, S7, and S8, respectively. The $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ of the samples in the frequency range of $2{\mathrm{-}}18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ are shown in Fig. 4c, d. The $\mu^{\prime}$ of S6 and S8 stayed at a value of 1.1 while S7 showed a relative higher value of 1.3 within the most of the measuring frequency range. In the frequency range of $2{\mathrm{-}}6\operatorname{GHz}$ , some peaks for $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ of S6 and S8 illus‑ trated the existent of magnetic resonance behavior [60, 61]. Furthermore, the $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ of S6 and S8 both decreased to nearly 0 when the frequency increased over $6\operatorname{GHz}$ , indicating the nature characteristic of the magnetic loss of S6 and S8 in the high frequency resulting from the Snoek′s limitation of Fe [62]. In addition, the S8 exhibited negative values of $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ in the frequency range of $15{\mathrm{-}}18\mathrm{GHz}$ , which was normally attributed to the radiation of magnetic energy [63–65]. According to Maxwell equations, this phenomenon could be caused by the magnetic energy released from internal magnetic field, which was induced by the motion of charges in carbon frameworks under the alternating EM field. Unlike the former ones, the $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ plot of S7 changed barely in the fre‑ quency range of $2{\mathrm{-}}14\operatorname{GHz}$ , and a peak appeared at $16\mathrm{GHz}$ which was also attributed to magnetic resonance in high frequency range [61]. According to the above analyses, the S7 sample might perform better impendence matching and electromagnetic energy dissipation ability than both S6 and S8.
The dielectric loss tangent $(\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\varepsilon}{=}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime})$ of samples was calculated and is shown in Fig. 5a. Overall, the value of $\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\varepsilon}$ showed an increasing trend with the increasing carboniza‑ tion temperatures from 600 to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This could also be attributed to the formation of rutile- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ and defects when the treatment temperature increased over $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Generally, in the frequency range of $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ , electronic polarization, Debye dipolar, and interfacial polarization and its related relaxation processes are the main sources of dielectric loss [9]. Firstly, the introduction of Fe nanoparticles, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles, and carbon nanosheets could form multiple interfaces, such as $\mathrm{Fe\mathrm{-}C}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ –C and ${\mathrm{Fe}}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ , to generate interfacial polarization and related relaxation process. The interfacial polarization process as mentioned could cause the Maxwell–Wagner effect [42, 66], which contributed to the enhanced dielectric loss of $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ composites. The dipolar polarization was also one of the main factors to dissi‑ pate the EMW energy, since the Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles and defects distributed on the carbon sheets would form dipoles.
In order to further understand the polarization behavior during the EM absorption process, the plots of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ versus $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ of all three samples are drawn in Fig. 5b–d. Based on the Debye relaxation, the relationship between $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ could be described as Eq. (1):
$$
\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
where $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ represent the real and imaginary part of permittivity, while $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}$ and $\varepsilon_{\infty}$ represent the static constant and the dielectric constant at infinite frequency, respectively. Hence, the semicircles in the plots of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ versus $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ could be signs for the presence of polarization process. As shown in Fig. 5b, there were several semicircles in the plot of S6, indicating the existence of polarization process. As for S7, it could be seen from Fig. 5c that multiple semicircles present in the frequency range of $6{\mathrm{-}}18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ , which suggested the polarization processes happened among most of the tested frequency range [36, 67]. The plot of S8 also exhibited a lot of small semicircles in the frequency range of $8{\mathrm{-}}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ as shown in Fig. 5d. In addition, the observed semicircles for S6, S7, and S8 mostly appeared in frequency range of
$10{-}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ , suggesting that the polarization and related relaxation process occupied the dominate position in dielec‑ tric loss mechanism at high frequency range. These polariza‑ tion processes may be caused by the diploes and interfaces existed in the $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ nanocomposites, which were beneficial to the EMW energy dissipation. Additionally, the conductive loss, which was mainly originated from the car‑ bon nanosheets and Fe nanoparticles, could be confirmed by the presence of the line tails in the plots of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ versus $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ [39, 42]. Furthermore, the conduction loss also plays an important role in EMW attenuation. As shown in Fig. 5b, c, the length of line tail extended as the temperature increased, indicating that the conductive loss was gradually strength‑ ened. Therefore, the above results revealed the existence of three main factors including dipole polarization relaxation, interfacial polarization relaxation, and conductive loss, which were in favor of the dielectric loss.
Besides the dielectric loss showing significant effects on EMW absorption, the magnetic loss also plays a key role in EMW absorption, which is theoretically originated from magnetic resonance, eddy current effect, and mag‑ netic hysteresis [3, 9]. It is reasonable to exclude magnetic hysteresis loss for the applied weak and relative highfrequency electromagnetic field [6]. Normally, the mag‑ netic resonance can be divided into natural resonance and exchange resonance. The natural resonance often happens in the low frequency range (lower than $10\:\mathrm{GHz}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), while the exchange resonance takes place at over $10\mathrm{GHz}$ range [3]. The calculated magnetic loss tangent $(\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\mu}=\mu^{\prime\prime}/\mu^{\prime})$ of samples is shown in Fig. 6a. The tan $\delta_{\mu}$ plots of S6 and S8 had a small peak at initial frequency range $(2{\-}4\operatorname{GHz})$ , which were assumed as a result of natural resonance, and they stayed around 0.05 in the frequency range of $5{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ . As for the $\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\mu}$ plot of S7, it fluctuated mildly at low frequency range and kept around 0.05 in the range of $5{\mathrm{-}}14\operatorname{GHz}$ . Then, the tan $\delta_{\mu}$ plot of S7 exhibited a peak at high frequency range $(14{-}18\mathrm{GHz})$ , whose apex exceeding 0.5 suggesting strong magnetic loss at related frequency range. Magnetic eddy current is another critical role for magnetic loss, which is inevitable in magnetic system. Such effect can be evaluated by $\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}$ , where $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ represent the real and imaginary part of permittivity and $f$ is the frequency of EM field, respectively. When there exists eddy current effect, the value of $\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}$ will be a constant as the frequency is changing [6, 36, 68]. Otherwise, there exists other magnetic loss mechanism in the system. As shown in Fig. 6b, the $\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}$ plots of S6 and S8 stayed as a constant from 8 to $18~\mathrm{GHz}$ , and the $\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}$ value of S7 was a constant at the range of $6{\mathrm{-}}14~\mathrm{GHz}$ . These results confirmed that the magnetic loss of Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\cal C}$ nanocomposites was mainly caused by eddy current effect within the tested frequency range. Additionally, the magnetic resonance, including natural and exchange resonance, also played an important role in magnetic loss based on the previous analysis of $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ .
The EM wave reflection loss $(R L)$ values of the Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\cal C}$ nanocomposites were calculated based on the measured data of EM parameters as Eqs. (2) and (3) [69–71]:
$$
R L\left(\mathrm{dB}\right)=20\log\left|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_{0}}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_{0}}\right|
$$
where the input impedance $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ of the absorber is given by the following equation:
$$
Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_{0}{\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}/\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}}\operatorname{tanh}{\left|j\left({\frac{2\pi f d}{c}}\right)\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\right|}
$$
where $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}},\mu_{\mathrm{r}},f,$ and d represent the complex permittivity and permeability of the EMW absorption materials, the related EM frequency, and the thickness of absorbers, respectively. Effective absorption bandwidth $(E A B)$ is defined as the fre‑ quency range related to the $R L$ value lower than $-10\mathrm{dB}$ , which indicates that more than $90\%$ of the EM wave energy could be absorbed in this range. Figure $_{7\mathrm{a-c}}$ illustrates the 3D maps of $R L$ data for S6, S7, and S8 at different thick‑ nesses over $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ . Besides, the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ (Fig. S7a, b), $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ (Fig. S7c, d)), and $R L$ results (Fig. S8) for Fe-MOFs and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}T_{x}$ (MXene) after carbonized at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Supporting Information as comparison. S6 showed a weak EMW absorption performance, which could be related to its low dielectric and magnetic loss, while a maximum $E A B$ of $6.5\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (from 11.5 to $18\:\mathrm{GHz}$ ) with a matching thickness of only $1.6\mathrm{mm}$ was achieved for S7 sample. It can be further tuned to own a strong EMW absorption ability by changing its thickness. For example, its $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ could reach − 41.5 dB at $10.1\ \mathrm{GHz}$ with an $E A B$ of $3.2~\mathrm{GHz}$ when thickness is
$2.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ , and a − 51.8  dB $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ (at $6.6~\mathrm{GHz}$ ) could be obtained at thickness of $3.0\mathrm{mm}$ . Despite that the dielectric loss of S7 was not the biggest comparing with that of S6 and S8, it achieved a better EMW absorption performance than S8 sample, which was ascribed to the more uniform distribution of Fe and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles on the laminated carbon surfaces, leading to a better synergistic effect of dielectric and magnetic response [35–37]. In the case of S8, it also showed a relatively wide $E A B$ of $5.0\mathrm{GHz}$ (from 12 to $17\mathrm{GHz}$ ) with thickness of $1.6\mathrm{mm}$ , as shown in Fig. 7c. Though the S8 performed bigger dielectric loss and similar magnetic loss compared with S7, its EMW absorption per‑ formance was not ideal, since EMW absorption needs the synergy effort of impendence matching and attenuation abil‑ ity for EM energy. In order to compare the EMW absorption performance obtained in current investigation and studies on MXene-based materials, the related results are summa‑ rized in Table 2. It is shown that the Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\cal C}$ composite derived at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ owned tunable, lightweight, and broad‑ band EM absorption performance compared with the other MXene and related materials reported in the studies.
Generally, two indispensable factors should be taken into consideration while aiming to achieve outstanding EMW absorption properties. The first is EMW attenuation capabil‑ ity of the absorption materials, and the second is the imped‑ ance matching. Normally, attenuation constant is used to qualify the EM energy attenuation ability of absorbers. Attenuation constants $\alpha$ of the samples, which are shown in Fig. 8a, can be calculated by Eq. (4) [67]:
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\sqrt{(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime})+\sqrt{(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime})^{2}+(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}+\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime})^{2}}}
$$
Both S7 and S8 showed strong EMW attenuation capa‑ bility in the high frequency range $(12{-}18~\mathrm{GHz})$ , while S6 exhibited a weak performance in the whole tested range $(2{\mathrm{-}}18~\mathrm{GHz})$ . It is obviously that S7 possessed the best attenuation capability compared to the other samples, which was beneficial to enhance EM wave energy attenuation. The uniform distribution of the Fe and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles and relative high graphitization degree of carbon sheets are the reasons for the high EM energy dissipation capability of S7. Conversely, the poor graphitization and formation of anatase- $\mathbf{\cdot}\Gamma\mathrm{i}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles in S6 prevented it to achieve a high attenuation constant. The normalized characteris‑ tic impedance $(Z=|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|)$ is used to evaluate the imped‑ ance matching of the samples, which is calculated based on Eq. (3). Normally, a good impedance matching means that the EMW could successfully enter the absorbers. To take sample thickness and measuring frequency into considera‑ tion, the 2D contour maps of $Z$ for S6, S7, and S8 are exhib‑ ited in Fig. 8b–d. As is well known, achieving good imped‑ ance matching requires that the value of $Z$ is equal or close to 1 [73, 74]. In Fig. 8b–d, the color of yellow represents the area in which $Z$ equals 1, which representing the excellent impedance matching. The mismatch of impedance in S6 was apparent since the appearance of large black area, as shown in Fig. 8b. Compared with S6, the impedance matching for
S7 and S8 showed a greatly improvement. Additionally, the yellow area of S7 was larger than that of S8, implying that S7 possessed a better impedance matching than S8. Over‑ all, the S7 owned stronger EM energy attenuation ability and better impedance matching than the other two samples, leading to lightweight and broadband EM absorption per‑ formance of S7.
A possible EMW absorption mechanism for the sandwichlike laminated $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ nanocomposites is illustrated in Fig. 9. Firstly, the multiple reflections between the conductive carbon layers and scattering among the Fe and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nano‑ particles had taken place as soon as the EMW penetrated in the absorbers. These phenomena would be helpful in enhanc‑ ing the EMW absorption efficiency. Additionally, the electron migration process including crossing and hoping over the con‑ ductive carbon sheets also played an important role in the EM energy attenuation. Secondly, dielectric loss produced by the polarization of dipoles and interfaces among the Fe, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and carbon would give a great contribution to transform the EM energy into thermal energy. In detail, the Fe, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparti‑ cles, and carbon sheets with different conductivity could form capacitor-like structure, such as the contact positions of carbon sheets, the interfaces of ${\mathrm{Fe}}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ , Fe–C, and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ –C. These capacitor-like structures could give rise to the accumulation and rearrangement of space charge, resulting in generating vast interface polarization processes in alternating EM field [3, 75, 76]. Thus, the interfacial polarization played a main role in the EM attenuation process for this sandwich-like $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ ternary nanocomposite. Moreover, the magnetic loss caused by natural resonance, exchange resonance, and eddy current were inclined to enhance the EM energy dissipation ability of the laminated $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ nanocomposites. In addition, the improved impedance matching was achieved by introduc‑ ing the rutile- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ and magnetic Fe nanoparticles through the in situ formation of Fe-MOFs followed by a suitable heat treat‑ ment. Based on the above discussion, it is highly expected that the laminated Fe& $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\cal C}$ nanocomposites could be a good candidate for high-performance EMW absorbing materials.
## 4  Conclusion
In summary, sandwich-like 2D laminated Fe $\kappa\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\mathrm C}$ nanocomposites with tunable EMW absorption perfor‑ mance have been synthesized from the MXene/MOFs precursors by the rapid microwave heating process fol‑ lowed by the carbonization process. The formation of Fe and rutile- $\mathbf{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles sandwiched by the twodimensional carbon nanosheets provided strong elec‑ tromagnetic energy attenuation and good impedance matching for EMW absorption. The $\mathrm{Fe}\&\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@{\bf C}$ nano‑ composites exhibited a broad effective absorption band‑ width of $6.5\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (from 11.5 to $18\:\mathrm{GHz}$ ) at a thickness of only $1.6~\mathrm{mm}$ and the minimum $R L$ value of − 51.8 dB at $6.6\:\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of $3~\mathrm{mm}$ , respectively. This work not only provides a good design and fabricating concept for the laminated metal and functional nanoparticles $\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{c}$ nanocomposites with good EMW absorption, but also offers an important guideline to fabricate various twodimensional nanocomposites derived from the MXene precursors.
